from revised_content import *
EXP=json.loads((ROOT/'work/expanded/results.json').read_text());SUP=json.loads((ROOT/'work/revision_detection/results.json').read_text());MM=EXP['metrics'];DD=SUP['methods'];PM=PILOT['disjoint_test']['metrics'];LM=RAGPILOT['metrics']
U=MM['Unfiltered'];H=MM['Two signals'];RR=MM['Rules'];BP=MM['BERT probe'];EXT=DD['ProtectAI DeBERTa'];MIX=DD['Mixed-window probe']
def pc(x):return f'{100*x:.1f}%'
def ci(x):return f'{100*x[0]:.1f}% to {100*x[1]:.1f}%'
ABSTRACT=f'''Retrieved evidence can contain instructions that redirect a language model away from its legitimate task. We evaluate a passage gate combining a frozen BERT probe with an explicit response-control cue. An overlap-audited BIPIA-derived study contains 37 clean records and 111 attacked passages; the combination detects 74 attacks, compared with 63 for rules and 22 for the probe. Supplementary tests compare a released DeBERTa classifier, 24 legitimate instruction-bearing paragraphs, mixed-window probe training and score sensitivity. A document-scoped RAG experiment crosses 33 distinct emails with four attack templates and two insertion positions, producing 264 attacked variants per method. With Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct, marker successes change from {U['attack_successes']} without filtering to {H['attack_successes']} with the combined gate. Clean correctness is {H['clean_correct']}/33, and attacked correctness is {H['attacked_correct']}/264. Source-cluster uncertainty and an uncached pilot separate evidence strength from runtime cost. The results identify complementary coverage, false-positive risks and evidence loss, supporting a bounded filtering benefit while showing that marker suppression and correct answers remain distinct outcomes.'''
ABSTRACT=ABSTRACT.replace('Supplementary tests compare a released DeBERTa classifier, 24 legitimate instruction-bearing paragraphs, mixed-window probe training and score sensitivity.','However, the combination rejects all 24 authored legitimate instruction paragraphs. An external DeBERTa baseline, mixed-window training and score sensitivity expose calibration trade-offs.').replace('The results identify complementary coverage, false-positive risks and evidence loss, supporting a bounded filtering benefit while showing that marker suppression and correct answers remain distinct outcomes.','The results show complementary attack coverage but poor benign-instruction transfer. Marker suppression, legitimate-content retention and correct answers require separate evaluation.')
section('Results',[
('Passage detection and external baseline',f"The original audited test contains 111 attacked passages and 37 unmodified records. Rules reject 63 attacks, the original probe 22 and the combination 74. The combined recall is 66.7%, with exact-email-cluster bootstrap interval {ci(PM['Two signals']['ci95']['recall'])}. All three reject zero original clean records. Table 2 adds the external classifier and mixed-window correction on this same test. Their distinct training data and calibration policies must be retained when interpreting the ranking."),
('TABLE','detection'),
('FIG','curves'),
('',f"The released ProtectAI classifier detects {EXT['tp']}/111 attacked passages and rejects {EXT['fp']}/37 original clean records at its fixed 0.5 threshold. Its F1 is {EXT['f1']:.3f} and AUROC is {EXT['auroc']:.3f}. These are measured passage-level results for our maximum-window wrapper. They establish a reference point beyond the local component ablations; they do not compare end-to-end generation, since this external condition is evaluated only as a detector."),
('',"The PR and ROC panels show the full score ordering, while Table 2 reports operating points chosen before held-out evaluation. Those views answer different questions. A high area under a curve can coexist with weak recall at a strict clean-retention threshold. Conversely, a useful discrete rule may have little threshold flexibility. The curves therefore supplement the fixed-threshold comparison rather than replacing it with whichever threshold looks best on the test set."),
('Legitimate instruction challenge',f"The unmodified email sample understates one important false-positive mechanism. On the authored instruction challenge, rules reject {DD['Rules']['benign_rejected']}/24 paragraphs, the original probe {DD['BERT probe']['benign_rejected']}/24, the combination {DD['Two signals']['benign_rejected']}/24 and ProtectAI {EXT['benign_rejected']}/24. Table 3 separates ordinary requests from cue-bearing benign text. The original 37/37 retention result should consequently be read as a result for that email sample, rather than broad preservation of all legitimate instructions."),
('TABLE','benign'),
('',"A printer's system message and a sender's request to reply to finance are benign in their documentary context. They can nevertheless match a rule for system-message impersonation or response control. This overlap is a task-alignment problem: syntactic instructions are not inherently malicious. The challenge deliberately places such vocabulary in legitimate settings, so its raw rejection count is diagnostic rather than an estimate of deployment false-positive prevalence."),
('Mixed-window training',f"The targeted correction fits {SUP['mixed_training_windows']} windows, including {SUP['mixed_positive_windows']} windows overlapping an inserted payload. Its validation-selected threshold is {MIX['threshold']:.6f}. Held-out recall changes from {pc(DD['BERT probe']['recall'])} for the original stand-alone-payload probe to {pc(MIX['recall'])}; clean false rejections change from {DD['BERT probe']['fp']} to {MIX['fp']} of 37. On the legitimate-instruction challenge, the corrected probe rejects {MIX['benign_rejected']}/24 paragraphs."),
('',"The result tests a concrete response to the training/inference mismatch. It should not be read as an isolated causal estimate for context mixing, because the corrected construction also changes window counts and label balance. The practical question is whether this one fixed correction improves the held-out operating point without introducing an unacceptable retention cost. The main generator experiment retains the original probe throughout, preserving the original ablation and preventing this supplementary comparison from becoming test-driven system selection."),
('',f"The corrected probe improves AUROC from {DD['BERT probe']['auroc']:.3f} to {MIX['auroc']:.3f}, yet its selected threshold reduces recall. Better ranking therefore does not solve operating-point calibration on a small development sample. Its benign rejection count falls from 22 to 11, which is an improvement on this challenge but still leaves nearly half the legitimate instructions rejected. The evidence contradicts a simple claim that inserting payloads during training automatically fixes the weak probe."),
('Weight and threshold sensitivity',"Figure 3 shows the pre-specified cached-score analyses. For each weight, threshold selection uses audited validation scores alone. The threshold sweep instead holds the weight fixed and displays possible test trade-offs descriptively. Plateaus are informative: if calibrated methods produce identical rejection sets over several weights, the apparent continuous formula does not yield a continuously different operational defence. The primary weight remains 0.75 regardless of the sweep."),
('FIG','sensitivity'),
('TABLE','sensitivity'),
('Expanded end-to-end security',f"The expanded crossing completes {EXP['requests']} logged requests: 33 clean and 264 attacked conditions for each of four methods. Without filtering, the marker appears in {U['attack_successes']}/264 responses. Counts are {RR['attack_successes']}/264 for rules, {BP['attack_successes']}/264 for the probe and {H['attack_successes']}/264 for the combination. Retrieval exposes the marker in {U['exposed_n']}/264 attempted attacks. The combination retains marker-bearing evidence in {H['marker_retained']} variants. This separates retrieval exposure, filtering and generator response."),
('TABLE','expanded'),
('',f"The combined ASR is {pc(H['asr'])}, with a source-email cluster interval of {ci(H['asr_cluster_ci95'])}. Relative to unfiltered retrieval, its paired ASR change is {100*H['paired_asr_change']:.1f} percentage points, with interval {100*H['paired_asr_change_ci95'][0]:.1f} to {100*H['paired_asr_change_ci95'][1]:.1f} points. The number of source emails experiencing at least one successful variant is {H['emails_with_any_success']}/33 for the combination, with Wilson interval {ci(H['any_success_wilson95'])}. These source-level counts prevent 264 correlated variants from being presented as 264 independent documents."),
('',"The crossed design supports a cleaner position/template comparison than the pilot's one assigned attack per email. Every cell now contains the same source emails. Per-cell outcomes are retained in the machine-readable results, including unsuccessful attacks. The remaining constraint is the fixed four-template bank: crossing more positions and documents broadens its execution coverage but does not add new attack strategies or an adapting adversary."),
('Utility and answerability',f"Clean correctness is {H['clean_correct']}/33 ({pc(H['clean_accuracy'])}) with the combination, compared with {U['clean_correct']}/33 without filtering. Under attack the respective counts are {H['attacked_correct']}/264 and {U['attacked_correct']}/264. For combined clean responses, {H['answerable_correct']}/{H['answerable_n']} amount-answer cases and {H['unknown_correct']}/{H['unknown_n']} unanswerable cases are correct. Aggregate utility therefore needs the answerability breakdown: copying a visible amount can still be wrong when the requested relation is unsupported."),
('',f"The combination uses an empty-context fallback in {H['attacked_abstentions']} attacked variants and {H['clean_abstentions']} clean cases. That behaviour prevents a marker at the cost of abstaining from some tasks. A correct UNKNOWN on an unsupported query is useful; returning UNKNOWN because the filter removed necessary evidence is a different mechanism. The raw retained-chunk identifiers and ideal labels make that distinction inspectable even when the aggregate accuracy is unchanged."),
('FIG','tradeoffs'),
('Computation and measured latency',f"The original uncached 16-email pilot provides the comparable cost measurement. Median gate times are {LM['Rules']['gate_median_ms']:.3f} ms for rules, {LM['BERT probe']['gate_median_ms']:.3f} ms for the probe and {LM['Two signals']['gate_median_ms']:.3f} ms for the combination. All appearances of these values use the same source and precision. Median clean request times are {LM['Unfiltered']['clean_request_median_ms']:.0f} ms without filtering and {LM['Two signals']['clean_request_median_ms']:.0f} ms with the combination. A linear head is cheap to fit, but online encoder inference adds substantial filtering work."),
('TABLE','latency'),
('',f"The expanded execution performs {EXP['actual_generator_calls']} fresh generator calls, reuses {EXP['generation_reused']} identical question/evidence responses and returns {EXP['empty_context_fallbacks']} empty-context fallbacks. These counters sum to 1,188 decisions. Reused generations have explicit origin keys; they supply the same deterministic answer, not a new latency observation. The mixed-window and external-model runs are kept outside the original pilot's timing sample. Wall-clock values remain conditional on the available CPU and background load."),
('Error analysis',old(8,3)),
('',old(8,4)),
('',"These pilot examples are retained as concrete mechanisms, not silently relabelled as newly observed expanded cases. They show two different risks: a model can ignore the attack marker yet hallucinate an answer, and a gate can remove a mixed chunk containing useful payment evidence. The expanded study tests whether the aggregate pattern persists over a complete fixed crossing, while the examples explain why neither ASR nor detection recall can replace a legitimate-task metric.")])
section('Discussion',[
('What the ablation establishes',"The two-signal construction is interpretable because its components and decision rule are fixed. At the original operating point it supplies complementary passage coverage beyond the rule and probe. The generator crossing evaluates the consequence of that coverage through actual answers. The stronger practical comparison includes the rule's low cost, the external classifier's measured behaviour and the legitimate-instruction challenge. A method earns its complexity through the joint trade-off, rather than through a single larger detection count."),
('',"The benign challenge changes the interpretation of clean retention. Ordinary email records do not test every vocabulary that a detector might mistake for an attack. Cue-bearing negatives reveal how a response-control rule can confuse documentary instructions with an instruction directed at the assistant. A passage-only classifier has no direct representation of the legitimate question or the sender/recipient relationship. Conditioning the detector on the task is therefore a substantive future direction supported by the observed ambiguity."),
('Threats to validity',"The source texts are de-duplicated by exact equality; near duplicates or shared email boilerplate may remain. The post-run overlap audit improves the analysis but was not independently pre-registered. Threshold calibration has only 14 clean records and therefore coarse resolution. The expanded source set contains 33 emails, and its attack templates are reused across all of them. Source-level intervals reflect document variation under those templates; they cannot quantify uncertainty over unseen attack families."),
('',"The generator is a small instruction-tuned model on a narrow numeric QA task. Its difficulty with unanswerable questions can dominate task accuracy, and the amount-set scorer does not establish faithful reasoning. External training contamination is only partly observable. The corrected probe changes contextualisation together with fitting-window composition. Runtime libraries differ between the pilot and extension. These constraints bound transfer claims without changing the recorded comparisons."),
('Practical implications',"Finer removal units could preserve facts that whole-chunk rejection discards, but they would need a separate answer-level utility check. A rule-first cascade, a smaller encoder or corpus-side feature caching could reduce inference cost; their threshold and false-positive effects would also need measurement. RobustRAG's isolate-and-aggregate design offers another direction when stronger corruption assumptions and extra model calls are acceptable [16]. Adaptive attackers should be introduced before extending these findings to deployment [11], [18].")])
SECTIONS[-1][1].insert(1,('',"The central negative finding is the combination's rejection of all 24 legitimate challenge paragraphs. H1 receives support for attack coverage in the original test, but a useful security/utility operating point does not transfer to these instruction-bearing negatives. This prevents a general recommendation to deploy the current gate. The challenge also changes genre and length relative to the development emails, so the result identifies a concrete distribution shift rather than estimating a real-world rejection rate."))
section('Conclusion',[
('',f"A frozen BERT probe and explicit response-control cue provide complementary passage coverage in this implementation: the combination detects 74/111 attacks against 63/111 for rules and 22/111 for the original probe. A released classifier, legitimate-instruction negatives, a fixed training correction and sensitivity analyses make the comparison more informative than retention on ordinary emails alone. In the expanded RAG crossing, marker successes are {U['attack_successes']}/264 without filtering and {H['attack_successes']}/264 with the combination, while clean correctness is {H['clean_correct']}/33."),
('',"The main lesson is to evaluate filtering as part of an answering system. A lower marker rate can coexist with wrong answers, rejected legitimate instructions and loss of supporting facts. The original uncached execution also shows that neural inference carries a measurable cost. Future work should prioritise task-conditioned detection, evidence-preserving removal and adaptive evaluation across independent domains and stronger generators. The released scripts, configurations and raw responses make the current bounded findings inspectable and reproducible."),
('',"The current gate is not ready for general use: it rejects every paragraph in the authored benign-instruction challenge. Mixed-window training improves score ranking but fails to improve recall at its selected operating point. These negative results make task alignment and representative calibration data higher priorities than adding further signals. The external classifier's large email false-positive count likewise shows that a released detector must be evaluated on the target data rather than adopted on its model-card accuracy."),
('Author contributions',"Weiyi Xia was responsible for items 1–8: interpreting requirements; defining research questions, threat model and scope; reviewing literature; designing signals and baselines; auditing data and splits; implementing the BERT probe and calibration; implementing the RAG pipeline; and defining attacks and evaluation metrics. Xinye He was responsible for items 9–16: preparing models and running experiments; checking statistics and errors; drafting and revising all report sections; checking references, provenance and reproducibility; preparing slides and speaker notes; and reviewing AI-assisted code, analyses and text. Both authors share responsibility for items 17–18: rehearsal, recording and MP4 assembly, final consistency checks, contribution reporting and submission preparation. Recording and submission responsibilities are not statements of completion."),
('AI assistance',"Generative AI was used for requirement extraction, code development and execution, experimental analysis, and drafting and revising the report, slides and speaker notes. The contribution allocation above records the authors' declared responsibilities and is not an estimate of unaided work. AI-generated technical material and wording require author verification. This disclosure is retained so that the use of assistance remains explicit.")])
